description:
persona-grpc/src/service.rs:219-221:

    // Get the local trust bundle's public key for verification.
    let pub_key_der = self.signer.public_key_der();
    let decoding_key = DecodingKey::from_ec_der(pub_key_der);

The comment says trust bundle. The code reads the public key straight off `self.signer` — the process's own ephemeral keypair (persona-core/src/signer.rs:55-61, 'never persisted, discarded when the process exits'). `self.bundles`, the actual TrustBundleStore, is not consulted, even though it is right there on the same struct (service.rs:18) and fetch_jwt_bundles() twelve lines up does read it.

What goes wrong: the comment describes the behaviour someone would expect from a SPIFFE Workload API — validate against the configured trust bundles, which is what makes federation and validating another party's SVID possible. The code can only ever validate a token this exact process instance signed; after a restart it rejects its own previously issued tokens. A reviewer skimming for 'does validation use the trust bundle' reads the comment, sees the word 'bundle', and moves on.

Fix: if validating only self-issued tokens is the intended scope for now, say that — replace the comment with something like 'only self-issued SVIDs are validatable: the ephemeral signing key is the sole verification key until federated bundles are populated (persona-rbu)', and preferably make it a ponytail: comment naming the upgrade path, matching the convention used elsewhere in this file (service.rs:155, service.rs:187, service.rs:247). If validating against the bundle store is intended, that is a functional gap and needs an issue rather than a comment.
